## Proof

Let $X_i = Z_i/\lambda$. Then $(X_i)$ are i.i.d. exponential with rate $1$ (mean $1$), and
$$\frac{W_n}{\lambda\log n}=\frac{\min\{Z_1,\dots,Z_n\}}{\lambda\log n}=\frac{\min\{X_1,\dots,X_n\}}{\log n}=: \frac{M_n}{\log n}.$$

**Step 1: $M_n \to 0$ almost surely.**

For any $\varepsilon>0$,
$$P(M_n > \varepsilon)=P(X_1>\varepsilon,\dots,X_n>\varepsilon)=e^{-n\varepsilon}.$$
Since $\sum_{n\ge 1} e^{-n\varepsilon}<\infty$, the Borel–Cantelli lemma gives
$$P(M_n > \varepsilon \text{ infinitely often})=0.$$
Hence $M_n \le \varepsilon$ for all sufficiently large $n$, a.s. Since $\varepsilon>0$ is arbitrary, $M_n\to 0$ a.s.

**Step 2: $M_n/\log n \to 0$ almost surely.**

We have $M_n\to 0$ a.s. and $\log n\to\infty$. For any $\varepsilon>0$, eventually $M_n<\varepsilon$ and $\log n \ge 1$, so
$$0 \le \frac{M_n}{\log n} < \varepsilon$$
for all sufficiently large $n$, a.s. Thus $M_n/\log n\to 0$ a.s.

**Step 3: Conclusion.**

Since $M_n/\log n\to 0$ a.s.,
$$\liminf_{n\to\infty}\frac{W_n}{\lambda\log n}=\liminf_{n\to\infty}\frac{M_n}{\log n}=0 \quad\text{a.s.}$$
Therefore
$$P\!\left(\liminf_{n\to\infty}\frac{W_n}{\lambda\log n}>1\right)=P(0>1)=0.$$

$$\boxed{P\!\left(\liminf_{n\to\infty}\frac{W_n}{\lambda\log n}>1\right)=0}$$

